WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
-Welcome back. Welcome back.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.200
-God, I might as well move in.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.033
-I love having you here.
I know.

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:06.367
How is your daughter?
How is she doing?

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:08.901
-She's pretty great.
Marlowe. She's 10.

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:10.367
-She's 10?
-It happens like that.

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:11.801
-Really does.
-It really does.

00:00:11.801 --> 00:00:12.934
But she's amazing.

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:14.767
-We spoiled her
last time she was here.

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:15.968
-Yeah.
-She came with you.

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:17.868
But we had puppies on the show.
-Oh, my God.

00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:19.834
-And she was like...
And she was like, "What?"

00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:21.267
And we put her
and she just stayed in her room

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:22.767
with, like, 15 puppies.

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.167
She's like, "This is
the best show ever."

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.334
-I tell her I'm going to work,
like, every time she comes,

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:29.167
she's like, looking at me --
"This is not work."

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:30.834
-Yeah, this is play.
-Surrounded by puppies.

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:32.167
-Yeah, we're playing
with puppies.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.167
-"You can't fool me."
-Are you still doing the thing

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.334
where you play an album
a week for her?

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.868
-So, we did that
for a long time,

00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:39.300
and now there is no way on Earth

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.334
that I could make her
sit through an album a week.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:43.367
It's actually -- She's got
great music taste,

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.734
and I would like to think
that I had a hand in that

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.167
through the early...
-She's taking over now.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:48.801
-She's taken over,
and it's a role reverse.

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:50.534
She actually educates me.

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:53.067
-She tells you about her...
-About current popular music,

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:54.834
because I'm stuck in the --
I don't know.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:56.334
-Yeah.
-Another century.

00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:58.767
-Yeah, but, so who has she
turned you on to?

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.033
-It was a lot of Taylor Swift.
A lot of...

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.701
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah, who we love.

00:01:02.701 --> 00:01:04.834
-Yes!
-Yes, we love her.

00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:07.133
Olivia Rodrigo.
-Yes.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.901
-You know,
and sort of quite emo...

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.968
emo...pop?
-Yeah.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:13.167
Have you taken her to a concert?
-Can you tell

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.834
I've got a 10-year-old?
I'm like, "Emo," you know.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:16.701
-Yeah.
-There's all these things.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.868
I mean, I'm not --
I'm not cool anymore.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:20.434
"Sus."
-Don't be sus.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.033
-Don't be sus.
-You guys are being sus,

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.033
a little bit, yeah.
-What's sus? Oh, no. "Shipping."

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:25.501
Have you heard shipping?
-No.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.601
-She's like, "So, he was
totally shipping her,"

00:01:27.601 --> 00:01:29.767
which means...
crushing on, I think?

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.767
-Oh.
-I'm like, "Shipping, you say?"

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.934
What is "ship"?
-"Shipping, you say?"

00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:35.267
-Is this nautical?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:37.300
I mean, like,
just not cool anymore, no.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.167
-No, you're totally cool.
What are you talking about?

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:41.167
-I think so.
But she does not.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.467
-Really?
-No.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:45.434
-But have you been --
have you been to a concert?

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:46.601
-So, I took her to Taylor Swift.

00:01:46.601 --> 00:01:48.834
That was her first concert.
She was about 5.

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:49.934
-Oh, my God.
-Or 6. Yeah.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.234
-Amazing.
-Amazing.

00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:53.601
And I took her and her cousins
and her best friend,

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:55.334
and I was definitely the oldest

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:58.000
and most enthusiastic member
of that audience.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.300
And we know Taylor,
very fortunately,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.300
so we got to go backstage
afterwards, which was a dream.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.033
But she was like,
"I saw you singing

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:06.300
every single word of my song."

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.434
Like, I was like...
-And she was a little worried?

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:09.634
-She was just like,
she's like,

00:02:09.634 --> 00:02:11.934
"You are a big fan."
I was like, "Yep, yep."

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:13.868
-Wow, so you were right
up close and like...?

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:15.467
-Yeah, I was just like, "Yeah!"
-Yeah.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.634
-No, I went -- I, like...
-♪ Shake it off ♪

00:02:17.634 --> 00:02:19.567
Yeah. Just yelling at her.
-Yeah.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.868
-I don't know what to do
at concerts at all.

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:23.467
-If you're with your kids,
you cannot dance.

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:25.067
You cannot look happy.
-No.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.667
-You cannot sing every word.

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:29.400
-What was your first concert?
Do you remember?

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.534
-My first concert
was Oasis in 1996.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:34.033
-What?!
[ Audience cheers ]

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.367
-It was.

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:37.234
I took my best friend

00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:38.734
and we went to Earl's Court,
and it was like,

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:41.634
it's an iconic gig that we like,
snuck out and went to.

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:43.534
I was 14 or something.
-Oh, my God.

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:45.701
-But I was, I was convinced
that they looked at me,

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:46.934
that Liam Gallagher
looked at me in the eye.

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:48.534
-Yeah.
-I was like, I know he saw me.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.334
I was so in love with him.

00:02:50.334 --> 00:02:52.033
-Oh, my gosh. Yeah.
"We're going to get married."

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:53.234
This is so cool.
I can't believe.

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:55.234
-It was on.
It was on from then.

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:56.767
Actually, my mother then met
Noel Gallagher

00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:58.100
in Harrods or something.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.601
Like, he was shopping
and she went up and she went,

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:01.000
"My daughter is going
to marry Liam."

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.434
He's like,
"I don't think so, actually."

00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:03.634
"I don't think she is."

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:05.000
And now he's one
of my best mates,

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.367
which I will tell everybody.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.734
And I did drop that name
and I will continue to do so.

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:10.934
-Noel Gallagher
-So now I'm Team Noel.

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:12.601
-I love...
Yeah, I love Noel Gallagher.

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.100
-Yeah, he's the best.
-I love both of them. Yeah.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.667
-And his band's amazing.
The new stuff is so fantastic.

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:18.033
Yeah.
-Yeah, they're...

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.934
That's a great
first concert to go to.

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:22.934
-Yeah.
-My first was Weird Al Yankovic.

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:24.501
-Oh, my God.
That is so on-brand.

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:26.000
-It's perfect, right?
-I've never loved you more.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.334
-It's kind of perfect.

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:28.501
-Of all the things
you could have said,

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:29.834
that's like, it's perfect.

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:30.968
-It was perfect.
It was my favorite. Yeah.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:32.300
-The rest is history.
Here you are.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.534
-And Oasis is perfect
for you, too.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:35.534
This kind of fits.
-Yeah.

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:38.167
-I know you're not a stranger
to performing onstage.

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:40.200
You were in "Cabaret," singing.
-Yes.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.467
-And you --

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:42.934
I don't know if we've told
the story on the show.

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:45.067
It makes me laugh.
It's sad, but it's funny.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:47.200
-It's sad.
I was doing "Cabaret,"

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.901
and the big number
in "Cabaret" is,

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:51.400
funnily enough,
a song called "Cabaret."

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.400
And the way
that we staged it

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.901
was like one spotlight
in the eyes.

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:57.100
So it's like you're blind.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.367
It goes to a blackout
and you're blind.

00:03:58.367 --> 00:03:59.701
The only way to get offstage
is to stand there

00:03:59.701 --> 00:04:01.934
with your arms out
and be led off

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:04.033
because the back wall moves in
for the next scene.

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:06.534
However, one night,
the man did not show up,

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:09.067
and I heard the wall move
and I was like,

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.868
I can't be stumbling around
in the platform.

00:04:10.868 --> 00:04:11.968
It's going to ruin the show.

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:13.734
So I ran in the direction
of offstage.

00:04:13.734 --> 00:04:16.300
I slammed...
Alan Cumming was the emcee.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.734
He heard like a...
[ Claps ]

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:20.400
That doesn't...
Well, like...

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.167
It was a skull on...
Thank you.

00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:26.968
Yeah, a skull on metal,
and I felt, like, warm blood

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.734
and I was just like, "I've just
got to finish the show."

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.067
Climbed up the stairs.
I'm standing in this balcony,

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:32.901
blood dripping down.

00:04:32.901 --> 00:04:35.734
Eight stitches, got a nice scar.

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:36.968
And then the next day,
they were like --

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:38.367
there was a matinee,
and they were like,

00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:39.968
"Are you sure
you want to do the show?"

00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:42.200
And I was like...
I had the biggest black eye.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.300
I was like, "Just put on
more lashes!"

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.834
And Sally Bowles
has to, like, look at people

00:04:46.834 --> 00:04:48.300
in the audience
and kind of flirt with them.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.767
And the men were hor-- I mean,
it was honestly like

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:54.501
I literally had like half an eye
that was black and...

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:57.100
and like loads of...
It was just not okay.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.434
-Oh, I think that's so funny.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:04:59.567
Come on.
See, that's a trouper, though.

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:00.634
-War wounds.

00:05:00.634 --> 00:05:02.334
-Yeah, that's a professional.

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:04.667
That's what we do.
-The show must go on.

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:06.634
-The show must go on.
-Yeah.

00:05:06.634 --> 00:05:08.400
-Let's talk about this show.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.133
"Extrapolations."
It's on Apple TV+.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.000
Meryl Streep, by the way,
plays your mom.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.367
I don't want to spoil anything.

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:16.868
But is that the reason,
one of the reasons you did it?

00:05:16.868 --> 00:05:19.033
-I mean, I think
if I got -- I got an email

00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:22.033
from Scott Z. Burns and it said,
"I would love you to do this."

00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:23.367
We'd worked together
on "The Loudest Voice."

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:24.534
He'd directed
an episode of that.

00:05:24.534 --> 00:05:25.801
So we were friends.

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:27.834
And then there was a P.S. --
"Meryl Streep is playing

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:29.634
your mother,"
at which point I was like,

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:31.400
"I don't need to read it.
I'm in."

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.534
You know, that's a bucket list,
isn't it, for any of us?

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:34.567
-Oh, my gosh.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:35.734
-Like to even be
in the room.

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.067
-What was she like?

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:38.834
-Oh, she's awful.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:40.767
Really, no, she's the best.

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.267
I mean, she's the best.

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:44.367
She's like everything
that you would want and more.

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:46.501
-I know you're you,
but do you get nervous

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:47.801
when you're next
to Meryl Streep?

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.033
Do you go, like...

00:05:49.033 --> 00:05:50.868
-Yes, let alone having
to look her in the eyes

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:52.200
and, like, act?
-Yeah.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.834
-'Cause all you want to do
is watch what she's doing.

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:56.601
She's so brilliant, which is
news to everyone, I know.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:05:59.267
She's a really great actor.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:01.000
-At the end of every episode,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.400
there's a screen
where people can go on a website

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.067
and get more information
about climate crisis.

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:06.567
And that website is...

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.234
-Yes.
-So go there, check it out,

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.100
see what you can do
to help the climate.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.267
The series finale
came out today.

00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:16.400
I want to show
everyone a clip.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.834
Here's Sienna Miller
in "Extrapolations."

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.167
Take a look.

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:20.734
-Dr. Shearer,

00:06:20.734 --> 00:06:24.167
were you paid for your work
at Menagerie2100?

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:25.434
-Yes.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.434
-Depressing work, I'm sure.

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:30.667
All that saying goodbye.

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:32.834
Yes?

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:35.000
That explains
why you receive

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.300
mood-stabilizing therapeutics
from Alpha.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.434
Ms. Shearer relies on Alpha for
her retirement and her medicine.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:45.067
What if her ongoing depression
has caused her to blame

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:46.934
the medicines
that Alpha provides her with?

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:50.067
-I know you want people
to think I'm crazy, Mr. Turner,

00:06:50.067 --> 00:06:53.667
and maybe --
maybe I am.

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:57.000
Maybe that's what climate change
is doing to all of us.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.601
Some of us are crazy
because we look at the world

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:01.334
and all we have lost,

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:04.667
and we still have hope.

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.701
-Come on, bud.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:08.300
You're fantastic.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.634
Who's better?
Sienna Miller!

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:11.434
Who's better than Sienna Miller?

00:07:11.434 --> 00:07:14.934
"Extrapolations" is streaming
now on Apple TV+.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.300
We'll be right back
with more "Tonight Show."

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.467
Stick around, everybody.

